WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.836
-Our next guest is a very
talented actor and comedian

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.904
you know from his work
on "Single Parents"

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:06.205
and "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:09.375
He is now back on Broadway
starring in "Spamalot,"

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:10.876
which is playing
at the St. James Theatre.

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:13.612
Please welcome back to the show
our good friend Taran Killam,

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:14.747
everybody.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:16.082
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:39.605 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.473
Welcome back.
-Hi, my friend.

00:00:41.473 --> 00:00:43.809
-Thank you for being with us
on your off night.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:45.111
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.747
And not an off night.
We're -- Monday shows.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.149
-Monday shows?
-Monday shows.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.951
So buy a ticket to "Spamalot."
It's very funny.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.319
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.887
-Now, I will say "Spamalot,"

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.322
when it first came to Broadway,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.991
I mean, I think like you loved
the film so much.

00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:01.427
-My whole life.
-Did not know what to expect
from the play.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:03.563
It was above and beyond
my wildest dreams.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:04.930
Did you see it back
in the day?

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.933
-I never got to see it.
I knew the album very well.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.302
I knew the songs
that were original

00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:11.771
to the Monty Python films,

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.906
like "Knights of the Round
Table" and "Always Look

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:16.409
on the Bright Side,"
and then the new ones too.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.545
You know,
I live in Los Angeles now.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:20.780
-Yeah.
-I have two children

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.982
who are beautiful and say hello,

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.584
and I did not think
that I would be doing theater

00:01:25.584 --> 00:01:27.553
until they were like
kicked out of the house.

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:30.123
So I cannot believe
that not only

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:31.524
am I doing a musical
on Broadway again,

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:33.526
but it's a Monty Python musical.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.428
It's just like the perfect,
perfect job for me.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:38.264
-You have been very lucky
with the times

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.999
that you've dipped your toe...
-This is true.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:41.067
-...into Broadway.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.235
You did a run
on "Hamilton" as well.

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:45.271
-Yes.
-"Hamilton" is a musical?

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.007
-It's a musical.
It's like, you know $10?

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.443
You ever seen $10?
-We've all seen $10.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.245
And so you got to play
King George in "Hamilton,"

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:55.714
which is a real juicy part.

00:01:55.714 --> 00:01:58.717
-It's the cushiest job
on Broadway.

00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:01.487
You sing the same song
three times,

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:04.724
you wear a funny cape
and everybody laughs at that.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.127
It was an extraordinary
experience.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:09.028
And I'm working
so much harder now.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.997
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:12.765
-I've made a terrible mistake.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.267
-Well, I want to ask about that.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.735
Yeah, you made a terrible
mistake,

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:18.804
but you have again, way more,

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:21.574
but these are the juicy
parts as well -- Lancelot.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.043
Yes, but you're also
the French taunter.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:26.746
-This is correct.
-One of the Knights Who Says Ni.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.714
-Yep.
-Among others.

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:32.852
-Yes.
-And it must be for somebody

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.687
who loved the movie...
-Yeah.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.756
-...it must have been
so easy to get off book.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.390
Not to make it easier
than it was, but...

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.661
-100%, I mean,
and the challenge, if anything,

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:44.898
is to not directly mimic Cleese.
-Right.

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:46.799
-But also honor it,
because I think

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.567
a lot of the audience
that's showing up

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:52.205
has expectations of like,
"I know how to say Ni."

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:56.242
-Yeah.
-"That's not how they say Ni."

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.711
But yeah, as we're doing
eight shows a week,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.481
like, you find new wrinkles
to kind of make it your own,

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.116
but you want to honor
the source material.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.551
And Eric Idle,
who co-wrote the show

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:08.054
with John Du Prez,
has been around.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.155
He's been hanging out
for the last week

00:03:10.155 --> 00:03:13.259
and giving notes
and compliments.

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:18.130
-Well, now I want
to say real quick like I...

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.166
So I texted you because
I had never met Eric Idle,

00:03:21.166 --> 00:03:22.534
and I met him recently

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:24.137
because he was in town
for opening night.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.772
-Yes.
-And I, you know,

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.407
I got a chance to tell him
how much I loved the show

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.274
when it first opened
and how excited I was.

00:03:29.274 --> 00:03:31.410
And he went out of his way
to say how great you were.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.878
And I know how much
it means to you.

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:35.114
I want to show the wide first.
You posted this on Instagram.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.450
So that's the great Eric Idle.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.251
But the look on your face when
you cut in.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:41.653
This is a man...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.653 --> 00:03:44.623
This is a man receiving
a compliment from his hero.

00:03:44.623 --> 00:03:47.026
-Yes. Yes.
-It's really great.

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:51.063
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Really, really lovely.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:52.631
-All of my castmates
were like,

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:54.800
"Oh, that's what it looks like
when you're sincere."

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.068
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:57.737
-They're like, "Everything
nice you've said to us

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:58.805
is a whole different face."

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.472
-"You phony!"
-Yeah.

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:03.042
You have received -- You
did receive great reviews.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.843
You opened last week.
Congratulations very much.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:08.113
And I know that's no small thing
when you are reviving a show

00:04:08.113 --> 00:04:09.515
that was so well received,

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:12.852
however many years ago,
15, 17 years ago.

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.988
-It does feel like
the bar has been raised

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:18.991
because, you know, Hank Azaria
originated the role in 2005,

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:21.327
and I'm a huge fan of his.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:26.866
But we received, in my opinion,
the highest plaudit possible

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:27.933
in our first week.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:30.369
We had to refund a ticket.

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:34.006
We had to rebook a ticket
because someone complained,

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.674
because the person
next to them was laughing

00:04:35.674 --> 00:04:38.377
so hard they wet themselves.
-Wow.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:43.649
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:45.751
[ Applause ]

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.286
-100% true story.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.455
What could you want more
from a Monty Python musical

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.457
than "this show is so funny,

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:54.927
people are literally
wetting themselves"?

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:58.364
-Wetting themselves enough
that their adjacent seat...

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.832
-Are aware of it.
-Aware of it.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:03.102
-There's a splash zone
to the urination.

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:05.771
-They didn't wet their seat.
They wet two seats.

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:09.242
-Exactly. You like it
so much you'll wet two seats.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.277
Joel de Ridder,
one of our crew members,

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:12.645
came up with
the perfect tagline.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:16.014
If we can put it on our marquee,
you're in for a treat.

00:05:16.014 --> 00:05:18.350
[ Laughter ]
-Very good.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.253
[ Applause ]
Very good.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.321
One might say, yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:25.324
-You know, and maybe
it was a little shame on me,

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:28.327
but I remember because
when I was growing up,

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.696
that was one of those VHS,
"Monty Python and Holy Grail,"

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.064
not every kid watched.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.300
I felt like maybe
the comedy kids watched.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.135
And so when I went
and saw the show on Broadway,

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.670
I was very taken
with the fact of like,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.072
oh, everybody was on
to this movie

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:43.609
because there is
that greatest hits feel of,

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:47.379
"oh, I know this scene
and I know how it's played,"

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:49.815
and people were
in the best mood,

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:51.884
and it just must feel
like such a gift

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.119
to be able to sort of be the new
steward of this material.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:55.521
-It is exactly that.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.390
Our first preview
was Halloween night.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.925
People showed up in costume.

00:05:59.925 --> 00:06:03.162
There are many moments where I'm
not able to get out my dialogue

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:05.864
before people finish it for me,
which is a treat

00:06:05.864 --> 00:06:08.868
because this show kind of allows
you to incorporate the audience.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.270
And so there's
a looseness there.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.672
But I think Josh Rhodes,
our director,

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.840
really did a great job
of freshening it up,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.976
of tightening it, but also
kind of giving us permission

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:22.081
to make it our own and
to update relevant references.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.617
And in moments where there's
some improv and looseness,

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:26.552
you know,
we're able to go off book.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.086
Leslie Kritzer,
who plays Lady of the Lake,

00:06:28.086 --> 00:06:31.490
who is mind-blowingly
talented and good,

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.158
steals the show
during "Camelot"

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:34.360
because she does
a whole lounge act

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.196
that's like 100% original
every night.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:38.530
-Wow.
-And I say that

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.999
so that hopefully people
will come back multiple times.

00:06:40.999 --> 00:06:43.035
-There you go.
-I think the show is that good.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:46.105
-Now that
we've finished the good news,

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:49.208
I know you are
a Los Angeles Rams fan.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:51.377
-Big, big R-- Whose house?!

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:53.345
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:55.514
Damn it!

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:58.951
-That was...
-Damn it!

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:00.986
-...the most -- I think that
might be the most

00:07:00.986 --> 00:07:03.489
over one's skis
a guest has ever been.

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:05.624
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:07.426
I like how you were like people

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:11.063
yell out your lines on Broadway.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:14.300
It must feel like
the opposite of that.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.535
-Yeah, that's why I did that.
-So I know on social media,

00:07:16.535 --> 00:07:18.236
I feel like if you're in town,
you go to the game.

00:07:18.236 --> 00:07:19.404
-Yes.
-Right?

00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:21.207
-I have season tickets

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.909
right in the front row.
I love it. Yeah.

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:25.244
-And it seems like,
you know, like this --

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:26.912
-Rampage?
-Rampage?

00:07:26.912 --> 00:07:29.081
-This guy --
-His name is Rampage?

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:30.482
-Rampage is a personal friend.

00:07:30.482 --> 00:07:32.050
He came to the show last night.
-Oh, wow. That's great.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:35.420
-People said, why is there a 6
1/2-foot ram walking the stage?

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.456
I said because he's my bro
and he knows that

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:40.425
whose house, Rams house.
-Oh, wow.

00:07:40.425 --> 00:07:41.560
That doesn't seem that...

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.562
-And yeah, now we're 4-6.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:46.131
-Yeah. Whose house?

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:49.435
Whoever came to play in this...
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:50.870
Those guys' house.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.538
-Yeah.
That's mostly true this season.

00:07:52.538 --> 00:07:54.072
-Yeah.
-Yeah, that's mostly true.

00:07:54.072 --> 00:07:55.541
-Since they've came back to
L.A.,

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.410
it's been very good for you.
-Yes. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.411
I'm very involved with the team.

00:07:59.411 --> 00:08:01.647
I was on the jumbotron

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:04.283
just on Sunday
when we beat the Seagulls.

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:05.451
[ Laughs ]

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:06.953
You are not a football crowd!

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:08.487
[ Laughter ]

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:11.490
-Well, they're also not
called the Seagulls.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.392
-They are in the Rams' house.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:17.596
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.596 --> 00:08:22.868
So it feels very confusing
to be at a Rams game.

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:25.271
So, alright. You were
in the "Spider-Verse."

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:28.473
-Yeah.
-What was your Spider-Man name?

00:08:28.473 --> 00:08:31.576
-I had the honor of playing
Web-Slinger in "Spider-Man:

00:08:31.576 --> 00:08:33.245
Across the Spider-Verse."

00:08:33.245 --> 00:08:34.947
Phil Lord, Chris Miller,
producers,

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:38.985
writers of of those films,
knew I was a huge fan,

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:40.786
but they also knew I can --

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.855
I'm cheating because I can see
what he's holding --

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:44.823
that I had written the comic
book issue for Web-Slinger.

00:08:44.823 --> 00:08:46.425
-So you had already written it?

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:48.194
-I had already written this,

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:49.862
this single issue of
Web-Slinger,

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:51.930
which is like Cowboy Spider-Man.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.565
And then Chris and Phil
called me up and they said,

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:55.868
"Hey, do you want to do
like five lines in the movie

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:57.636
and like one of the biggest
movies of the year?"

00:08:57.636 --> 00:09:00.506
And I thought about it
for a while.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:02.141
-Played hard to get.
-I played hard to get.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:04.510
Saw a couple Rams games!
-Oh, boy.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.912
-No? Okay. Yeah.
No, it was a dream.

00:09:06.912 --> 00:09:08.381
It was -- It was --
It's the coolest.

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:10.650
-I took my -- You know,
it's a two-parter.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.251
You know that because --
-I'm very aware. Yeah.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:13.953
-I don't have
to tell you anything.

00:09:13.953 --> 00:09:17.056
You're ahead of the game.
So I brought my boys to see it.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:20.659
And we saw it.
And I don't know if other people

00:09:20.659 --> 00:09:22.027
knew it was going to be
a two-parter.

00:09:22.027 --> 00:09:24.529
-Yeah, there's a lot of story
in that first chapter.

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:26.365
-There's a lot of story
in the first chapter.

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:29.468
And my kids,
they're just happy to see like

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:31.904
Spider-Man on the on the screen.
They're not...

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:33.805
But it ended and it was like,
you know,

00:09:33.805 --> 00:09:35.174
part two coming next year.

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:37.576
And the woman behind us
was like, "What the [bleep]?"

00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:38.644
[ Laughter ]
-Oh!

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:40.146
-And again, it's a Spider-Man.

00:09:40.146 --> 00:09:42.682
It's all kids.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:42.682 --> 00:09:44.616
-Yeah.
You wanted a "what the thwip."

00:09:44.616 --> 00:09:46.852
-Yeah, or just like, you know,

00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:48.854
just like,
take it with a grain of salt.

00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:50.689
-Like, "Well, I guess
we're coming back, honey."

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:53.458
-Yeah, you're going to come back
and see another movie.

00:09:53.458 --> 00:09:55.961
She's like,
"I can't go around the rest

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:59.131
of my Saturday
not knowing what happens."

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:01.133
-Yeah. Sorry.

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:03.402
On behalf of --
As the most important

00:10:03.402 --> 00:10:05.604
representative
of "Across the Spider-Verse,"

00:10:05.604 --> 00:10:07.473
let me just say, ma'am,
I'm sorry,

00:10:07.473 --> 00:10:11.276
but you won't be angry
when Part 2 comes out.

00:10:11.276 --> 00:10:14.713
-You were also in a remake
of "The River Wild."

00:10:14.713 --> 00:10:17.483
-Correct.
And we took away the "the."

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.484
-Oh, it's just "River Wild"?
-Just "River Wild" for us.

00:10:19.484 --> 00:10:21.086
-Oh, that's great.
That's so much better.

00:10:21.086 --> 00:10:24.156
-It's classier.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:26.992
It takes less time to get
to how wild that river is.

00:10:26.992 --> 00:10:29.061
-The "the" was my least
favorite part of the original.

00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:31.430
-Same, same, same. No.
It was amazing.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:35.134
And it was received very well
and was number two on Netflix

00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:36.635
at one point.
But I couldn't talk about it.

00:10:36.635 --> 00:10:37.937
So we were number two
on Netflix!

00:10:37.937 --> 00:10:40.038
-There you go.
Now you're making up for it.

00:10:40.038 --> 00:10:43.509
-But we shot in Hungary.
We shot in Budapest.

00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:46.611
Mostly.
That's where we were located.

00:10:46.611 --> 00:10:49.148
And I recreated one
of my favorite "Late Night

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:50.482
with Seth Meyers" segments.

00:10:50.482 --> 00:10:53.085
-You went day drinking?
-I went day drinking. By myself.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:54.353
-By yourself.

00:10:54.353 --> 00:10:57.189
So that's less fun than doing it
with another person.

00:10:57.189 --> 00:11:00.192
-Yeah, you'd think so but
honestly, I can't remember.

00:11:00.192 --> 00:11:02.828
-So is this a --
This is an ice bar?

00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:04.162
-That's where we started.
-Okay.

00:11:04.162 --> 00:11:07.199
-It's 11:45 a.m.
-Okay. Oh, wow.

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:08.567
And do you have to wear,
like, an ice coat?

00:11:08.567 --> 00:11:10.536
-Or else you'll die!

00:11:10.536 --> 00:11:11.737
-Oh, wow.

00:11:11.737 --> 00:11:13.338
And then this is what,
like, bar trivia?

00:11:13.338 --> 00:11:14.740
-This is bar trivia.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:19.678
Like a comic book-themed bar
there. That was just a bar.

00:11:19.678 --> 00:11:20.679
An aptly named bar.

00:11:20.679 --> 00:11:22.481
This is karaoke.
-Oh, wow.

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:23.682
-I met up
with a friend for karaoke.

00:11:23.682 --> 00:11:25.484
It's not as sad as it seems.

00:11:25.484 --> 00:11:27.619
-It's still pretty sad!
[ Laughter ]

00:11:27.619 --> 00:11:30.222
And then...
-Alright.

00:11:30.222 --> 00:11:31.990
And this is Rumpus.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.226
This is my favorite tiki bar
I've ever been to.

00:11:34.226 --> 00:11:37.195
If you find yourself
in Budapest, go to Rumpus.

00:11:37.195 --> 00:11:41.100
They have the Death Race
Challenge. And by this point --

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.434
-What is a Death Race Challenge?

00:11:42.434 --> 00:11:43.735
These drinks?

00:11:43.735 --> 00:11:46.472
-These drinks that are
increasingly more alcoholic.

00:11:46.472 --> 00:11:47.740
-Okay.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.008
-And if you win,
you get a little yellow token.

00:11:50.008 --> 00:11:52.444
-So this is what you do for
your alcohol poisoning?

00:11:52.444 --> 00:11:55.114
-Let's just say
I survived the Death Race.

00:11:55.114 --> 00:11:58.216
-Wow, Taran, you've had
such a big year.

00:11:58.216 --> 00:12:00.752
-Of all the accomplishments.
-That's the best one.

00:12:00.752 --> 00:12:02.521
-Yes.
-Hey. Thank you, buddy.

00:12:02.521 --> 00:12:04.456
Break a leg tonight.
-Thank you.

00:12:04.456 --> 00:12:05.724
-Taran Killam, everybody.

00:12:05.724 --> 00:12:08.360
You can see Taran in "Spamalot,"
which is playing at

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:09.561
the St. James Theatre on
Broadway.

00:12:09.561 --> 00:12:10.730
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

